## Context

The spec says "A blank line ends the header block" (`spec-erg-v1.md`).
The parser implements this literally: in `parseErgBytes` the `case
"headers"` arm transitions `section = "gap"` on the first blank line,
and every line in `gap` is silently discarded until `--- log ---`. So a
single stray blank line *inside* the header block makes every header
below it vanish.

This is a footgun, not a feature. `tickets/AGENTS.md` explicitly invites
agents to write `.erg` files by hand, and the natural instinct is to
visually group headers — a blank line after `Title:`, or before a
`Tag:`/`Blocked-by:` cluster. The tooling itself never emits such a
blank (verified: `insertClosedHeader` inserts after the last non-blank
preamble line; `removeTagLine`/`removeBlockedByLine` only delete lines).
The blank lines come from humans and LLMs authoring tickets directly.

Two observed failure modes, both reproduced against the committed
binary (2026-05-27):

1. **Silent data loss that masks real errors.** A ticket with

   ```
   %erg 0.1
   Title: demo
   Created: 2026-05-27
   Author: claude

   Tag: needs-human
   Blocked-by: 0002
   ```

   passes `erg validate` clean — the `Blocked-by: 0002` is dropped into
   `gap`, so rule 10 (unknown-ref) never fires even though `0002` does
   not exist. `Tag:`, `Blocked-by:`, and `Closed:` placed after a blank
   line are all swallowed this way; a stray `Closed:` after a blank
   would silently fail to close the ticket.

2. **Misleading "missing required header" errors.** A blank line after
   `Title:` makes `Created:` and `Author:` land in `gap`, so validate
   reports them missing — pointing the author at the wrong problem,
   since the headers are plainly right there in the file.

## Decision: three strictness tiers

Apply the Robustness Principle (already invoked in spec-erg-v1.md
"Postel's Law"), but split it three ways for an interior header blank:

- **Accept on read.** The parser tolerates blank lines within the header
  block: a blank line no longer terminates header parsing while
  subsequent lines still look like headers. Headers below the blank are
  parsed normally instead of being discarded. This fixes both failure
  modes above — refs/tags/closed are seen, required headers are found.
- **Autofix on write.** Any command that rewrites a ticket (`close`,
  `tag`, `untag`, and the `Blocked-by` removal in `close` step 3) strips
  stray blank lines from inside the header block as part of its edit, so
  files self-heal on the next mutation.
- **Sweep on migrate.** Autofix only fires on the *next* mutation, so
  hand-authored files would linger dirty indefinitely. `erg migrate`
  therefore normalises interior header blanks across the whole corpus in
  one pass — same preamble-bounded rewrite it already does for
  `Status:`/`Tags:`. This is the one command that proactively cleans
  every file, giving a corpus a single "make it clean now" lever.
- **Shout on validate.** `erg validate` (per-file, the pre-commit gate)
  does **not** stay silent: it prints a non-fatal warning to stderr for
  an interior header blank and still exits 0. Validate runs in the hook,
  so this is where an author actually sees the nudge at commit time.
  Precedent: validate already prints `WARNING:` lines to stderr (skipped
  files) without affecting the exit code.
- **Warn on check.** `erg check` emits a non-fatal warning (alongside
  `encodingWarnings`, `folderClosure`, etc.) when a ticket has a blank
  line inside its header block, so a corpus scan surfaces files that need
  a cleanup pass even if no command has touched them.

The blank stays a *hygiene* issue, not a hard format violation — accept
on read keeps `validate`/`check` at exit 0, consistent with how BOM/CRLF
are handled (normalised on read, surfaced as warnings, never fatal). The
difference from BOM/CRLF: validate shouts here too, not only check.

## Relevant files

- `src/go/erg.go` — `parseErgBytes`: the `case "headers"` / `case "gap"`
  state machine. The read-tolerance change lives here. Care needed: the
  blank line that *legitimately* ends the header block (the one before
  `--- log ---`) must still end it; only blanks followed by more header
  lines are absorbed. Distinguish "blank then header-shaped line" (tolerate)
  from "blank then separator / end of preamble" (terminate).
- `src/go/close.go` — `insertClosedHeader`; the close/untag rewrite path.
- `src/go/tag.go` — `removeTagLine`, tag insert path.
- `src/go/migrate.go` — `migrateFile` preamble rewrite: add interior-blank
  collapsing to the per-file rewrite so `erg migrate` sweeps the corpus
  clean in one pass (mark it a `changed` reason in `migrateResult`).
- `src/go/check.go` — add a `headerBlankLine` warning function, wire it
  into `cmdCheck` alongside the existing warnings.
- `src/go/validate.go` — `cmdValidate` must shout: detect the interior
  blank and print a non-fatal `WARNING:` line to stderr, keeping exit 0.
- A shared helper — one `collapseHeaderBlanks([]byte) []byte` plus one
  `hasInteriorHeaderBlank([]byte) bool` detector — feeds every tier
  (autofix paths, migrate sweep, validate shout, check warn) so the
  definition of "interior blank" lives in exactly one place.
- `spec-erg-v1.md` — the "A blank line ends the header block" sentence
  needs a clause: the first blank line *followed by a non-header line*
  ends the block; interior blanks are tolerated on read, normalised on
  write, warned on check.
- `tests/test_check.sh`, `tests/test_validate.sh`, `src/go/erg_test.go`,
  `src/go/validate_test.go` — coverage.

## Test

First failing test (read tolerance), Go:

```go
func TestParse_BlankLineInsideHeaderBlock(t *testing.T) {
    src := "%erg 0.1\nTitle: t\nCreated: 2026-05-27\nAuthor: a\n\n" +
        "Tag: needs-human\nBlocked-by: 0002\n\n" +
        "--- log ---\n2026-05-27T10:00Z a created\n\n--- body ---\n"
    e, _ := parseErgBytes([]byte(src), "0001-x.erg")
    if len(e.Tags) != 1 || e.Tags[0] != "needs-human" {
        t.Fatalf("Tag after interior blank was dropped: %v", e.Tags)
    }
    if len(e.BlockedBys) != 1 {
        t.Fatalf("Blocked-by after interior blank was dropped: %v", e.BlockedBys)
    }
}
```

Today this fails: `Tags` and `BlockedBys` are empty because both lines
landed in `gap`.

Shell (warn on check): a fixture with an interior header blank makes
`erg check` print a `WARN ...: blank line inside header block` line and
still exit 0.

Shell (shout on validate): `erg validate` on the same fixture prints a
`WARNING:` line to stderr **and exits 0** — assert both (the warning is
present, the exit code is 0, validate did not start failing).

Shell (sweep on migrate): `erg migrate <dir>` on a corpus containing a
ticket with an interior header blank rewrites that file so the blank is
gone, reports it in the migrate summary, and is idempotent (a second run
changes nothing).

Shell (autofix on write): `erg tag <id> needs-human` on a ticket with an
interior header blank produces a file whose header block has no interior
blank line afterwards.

## Spec sync (required — not optional)

This change alters parser-observable behaviour, so the docs must move in
the same PR. Per `spec-erg-v1.md`: "Any divergence between this document
and the `erg` binary must be resolved by aligning the specification with
the behavior." Landing the code without the doc update is a divergence
and must not merge. Three artifacts to keep in lockstep:

1. **`spec-erg-v1.md`** (normative) — the sentence "A blank line ends the
   header block" (§ Structure) is now wrong as written. Replace it with:
   the header block ends at the first blank line that is *not* followed
   by another header line; an interior blank (blank followed by more
   header-shaped lines before `--- log ---`) is tolerated on read,
   normalised on write, swept by `erg migrate`, and surfaced as a
   non-fatal warning by both `erg validate` and `erg check`. Also extend
   the "Postel's Law" section to name these tiers explicitly, since it
   currently reads "strict on write" without qualification.
2. **`pep-erg-v1.md`** (rationale) — record *why* the literal
   "blank-ends-header" rule was relaxed (silent dropping of
   `Blocked-by`/`Closed` masked validation errors), so the decision is
   traceable and a future reader does not "fix" it back.
3. **`docs/erg-manual.md`** — update the `erg check` warnings list to
   include the interior-header-blank warning, and bump the
   "Generated-from" binary header to the rebuilt binary (the manual
   carries a binary-hash provenance line, cf. commit d1dcb0d).

Acceptance: a reviewer reading only `spec-erg-v1.md` must be able to
predict the parser's behaviour on an interior header blank without
reading the Go source. The closed tickets 0096 ("align spec/pep/manual")
and 0127 ("spec/pep alignment gaps") establish that these three move
together — do not break that.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Headers below an interior blank line are parsed (read tolerance);
      both failure modes from Context are gone — rule 10 fires on the
      dropped `Blocked-by`, required headers are found.
- [ ] `close`/`tag`/`untag` strip interior header blanks when they
      rewrite a file.
- [ ] `erg migrate` sweeps interior header blanks across the whole
      corpus in one pass, reports them, and is idempotent.
- [ ] `erg check` warns (non-fatal) on interior header blanks.
- [ ] `erg validate` shouts: prints a non-fatal `WARNING:` to stderr on
      an interior header blank but still exits 0 (accepts the file).
- [ ] **Spec sync done in the same PR**: `spec-erg-v1.md` sentence +
      Postel's-Law section updated, `pep-erg-v1.md` rationale recorded,
      `docs/erg-manual.md` warnings list + Generated-from header bumped.
      Spec and binary must not diverge on merge.
- [ ] Go + shell tests green; rebuilt binary committed at `tickets/erg`.

## Notes

- "Interior blank" = a blank line that appears before the header block
  has otherwise ended (i.e. there are still header-shaped lines before
  `--- log ---`). The single intended blank between the last header and
  `--- log ---` is normal and stays.
- Scope is the *header* block only. Blank lines in the log section are
  already skipped; blank lines in the body are meaningful markdown and
  untouched.
- Feature-freeze note: this is filed as a correctness fix (silent
  dropping of `Blocked-by`/`Closed` masks validation errors and can
  leave a ticket falsely "open"), which the current milestone allows.
  The autofix/warn tiers are the minimal, consistent way to land the
  read fix without a format-breaking change.
